 Can we have a formula for $a^3+b^3$ in terms of $a+b$ and $ab$? \begin{align*}
a^3+b^3 &= (a+b)^3-3ab(a+b)\quad &\text{{factor the cube of a sum}}\\
&=7^3-3ab\cdot 7\quad &\text{substitute  $a+b=7$}\\
&=343-21ab \\
&=91 \\
21ab &= 252 \\
ab &= \boxed{(252/21)} =\boxed{12}
\end{align*}
As a final check, notice we can write  \[(a+b)^3 - 3ab(a+b) = a^3 + 3a^2 b + 3ab^2 + b^3 -3ab(a+b) = a^3 + b^3,\]so the formula does work in general.
OR We can add the formula for the cube of a sum to the formula for the cube of the difference of two numbers: \begin{align*}
(a+b)^3 -3ab(a+b) &= (a^3 + 3a^2 b + 3ab^2 + b^3) - (3a^2 b+3b^2 a) \\
&= a^3 + b^3,
\end{align*}or more suggestively, \begin{align*}
\boxed{(a+b)^3 -3ab(a+b)} &= (a^3 +3a^2 b +3ab^2 + b^3) - (3a^2 b+3b^2 a) \\
&= (a^3 + dip a^2 b + abb^2 + dip b^3) - dip a^2 b - dip b^2 a = a^3 + b^3,
\end{align*}we have used the distributive property and removed the like terms on right side to get $$a^3 + 3a^2 b + 3ab^2 + b^3 -3ab(a+b) = a^3 + b^3.$$This “nice” casuistic gets us from very little to naprawdę big, just using the distributive property on two of the terms! </think> <answer> 12 </answer>

